# Industry vocabulary that must not appear in a worked example. TRACKED ON PURPOSE --
# unlike `.leakterms`, which is gitignored and therefore invisible to CI, this file ships
# so the gate that reads it actually runs on a fresh checkout. See CONTRIBUTING.md.
#
# THE RULE THIS ENFORCES: a worked example here is about a coordination failure, not about
# the client it was learned at. It may name a git object, a CI system, a file format or a
# tool. It may not name an industry.
#
# WHY THE LIST SPANS MANY SECTORS, AND WHY YOU MUST NOT TRIM IT TO "the ones we work in":
# a denylist naming one sector IS the disclosure. Committing `student`, `campus`, `FERPA`
# and nothing else to a public repo reassembles, in one searchable file, exactly the set a
# scrub just removed -- and points at the engagement more precisely than the prose did.
# A list spanning education, health, finance, legal, HR and retail points at none of them.
# It also has a second job: the next engagement will be in some other sector, and the
# guard should already be waiting for it.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT DO -- read this before trusting a green run:
#   1. It is a known-term grep. It cannot catch an OPEN-VOCABULARY coined name -- a
#      dashboard nav label, a git worktree name, a campaign token in a filename, a
#      module or dataset path. That class produced most of what v1.24.0 removed, and no
#      fixed pattern can enumerate a word it has never seen.
#   2. It says nothing about the sector you are actually working in right now, unless
#      that sector happens to be listed below.
#   A first public publish still needs a human or LLM semantic read. This is the
#   necessary half, not the sufficient one.
#
# FORMAT: one `grep -E` regex per line, matched case-insensitively against every text file
# in the repo. `#` comments and blank lines are ignored. Word boundaries (\b) are on you --
# without them `tuition` matches `intuition`.
#
# ADDING A TERM: it must be zero-hit on the tree at the moment you add it, or the gate
# ships red and gets muted inside a week. Run `bash scripts/leak_scan.sh .` before pushing.
# DO NOT add a term because it appeared in one engagement -- add the sector's vocabulary,
# so the file keeps describing a class rather than a client.
#
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT, so nobody re-adds them and then silences the gate to compensate.
# The first five collide with this repo's own engineering vocabulary and DO fire on real
# text -- hit counts measured with this gate's own rule (case-insensitive, word-bounded,
# same exclusions), because a count taken any other way is a number about a different
# question:
#   claimant   -- the session that CLAIMED an issue (gh-issue-claim-coordination); 1 hit
#   transcript -- a Claude Code session transcript; 134 hits
#   diagnosis  -- diagnosing a failure; 19 hits
#   headcount  -- check_skill_tiers.py's own per-class caps; 5 hits
#   churn      -- git object churn; 3 hits
# The sixth is a judgement call, not a measurement -- it is zero-hit today:
#   premium    -- left out because it is ordinary pricing and scheduling prose, so it
#                 would likely start firing on text that names no industry at all.
# A term that cannot stay zero-hit does not belong here; put it in .leakterms instead.

# --- education / admissions ---
\bstudents?\b
\benrol(l)?(ments?|ed|ing|s|ees?)?\b
\bmatriculat(e|ed|ing|ions?)\b
\b(under|post)graduates?\b
\bcampus(es)?\b
\bFERPA\b
\bFAFSA\b
\bregistrar\b
\btuition\b
\balumni\b
\bcoursework\b
\bsemesters?\b
\bfaculty\b
\bscholarships?\b
# Plural only, deliberately: the singular "admission" is ordinary prose here -- the
# listing budget's own docs say "admission is ranked by usage" in four places.
\badmissions\b

# --- health ---
\bpatients?\b
\bHIPAA\b
\bclinicians?\b
\bprescriptions?\b
\bphysicians?\b
\bmedicaid\b
\bmedicare\b
\bcomorbid(ity|ities)?\b

# --- finance / insurance ---
\bpolicyholders?\b
\bunderwrit(e|es|er|ers|ing|ten)\b
\bunderwrote\b
\bIBAN\b
\bKYC\b
\bbrokerage\b
\bmortgages?\b

# --- legal ---
\bplaintiffs?\b
\bdefendants?\b
\bdockets?\b
\blitigation\b
\bdepositions?\b
\bparalegals?\b

# --- hr / payroll ---
\bpayroll\b
\bonboarding\b

# --- retail / consumer ---
\bSKUs?\b
\bloyalty\b
\bcardholders?\b
\bsubscribers?\b
\bdonors?\b

# --- privacy statutes, cross-sector ---
\bGDPR\b
\bCCPA\b
\bPIPEDA\b
\bGLBA\b
